  "avisos": [
    "Patch test each new ingredient and each finished mixture on separate small areas of your inner forearm before facial use. Follow the intended contact time, rinse off rinse-off mixtures, and monitor for at least 24 hours. Allow longer testing for delayed reactions; a 24-hour check doesn't establish safety. Skip anything that causes itching, burning, redness, or swelling, and never test a known allergen.",
    "If you have sensitive skin, inflammatory acne, rosacea, dermatitis, another skin condition, or are pregnant, speak with a dermatologist before starting. Don't apply homemade mixtures to cuts, broken skin, or irritated areas.",
    "Use broad-spectrum SPF 30+ sunscreen every morning, rain or shine, following the label. Reapply at least every 2 hours outdoors and after swimming or sweating. Choose water-resistant sunscreen for outdoor activity, and use shade and protective clothing too. Homemade recipes provide no reliable sun protection.",
    "Rice Water Refresh: soak a fresh portion in the refrigerator, use immediately after straining, and discard leftovers. Don't ferment it. Stop if it leaves skin feeling tight. Refrigeration and a normal smell don't establish that a homemade mixture is safe.",
    "Rich Comfort Cream: this rich texture may not suit oily or acne-prone skin. Use a clean spatula and make a fresh single-use portion. Its original 30-day storage claim isn't verified.",
    "Aloe Comfort Gel: use packaged aloe vera gel with 100% pure aloe as its base, no added fragrance, dyes, or drying alcohol, and follow its label. Avoid raw aloe leaves and yellow leaf sap. Once mixed with other ingredients, make only a fresh single-use portion.",
    "Eye-Area Comfort Dab: use only a tiny amount along the outer orbital bone. Keep homemade mixtures off eyelids and away from lashes and eyes. Your usual fragrance-free eye product is an alternative. If stinging, redness, or tearing occurs, rinse with clean water and stop using it.",
    "Oat Cloud Mask: skip it if you're allergic to oats. If you're allergic to milk, use only a plain plant milk whose ingredients you tolerate. Don't substitute another known allergen. Grind oats finely, rinse without rubbing, and discard leftovers.",
    "Light Green Tea Toner: use plain, unflavored tea and keep the brew light. Let it cool completely before use. Make a fresh single-use portion and discard leftovers; a strong brew may feel drying.",
    "Flaxseed gel, the base for the Smooth-Feel Flaxseed Mask: use freshly made, fully cooled gel. Rinse before it dries completely and immediately if it feels tight, itchy, or uncomfortable. Any smoother appearance is temporary.",
    "DIY Hydrating Sheet Mask: use fresh liquid and clean cotton rounds, keeping them away from eyes, nostrils, and mouth. Don't sleep in the mask or let it dry on your skin. Discard the rounds and leftover liquid after use.",
    "Silky Flaxseed Serum: prepare only a fresh single-use portion and discard leftovers. Don't rely on smell, texture, or refrigeration to determine whether it is safe.",
    "Honey & Yogurt Comfort Mask: use plain, unsweetened yogurt and a little plain sunflower oil in place of olive oil. Skip the recipe if you're allergic to any ingredient. For oily skin, leave on for no more than 10 minutes, as tolerated. Rinse sooner if uncomfortable.",
    "Silky Finish Oil: this version uses plain jojoba oil in place of sweet almond oil. Patch test first. For oily skin, use just 1 or 2 drops on dry areas if needed. Keep it away from eyes and stop if it causes irritation or breakouts.",
    "Cool Morning Eye Compress: use clean, soft washcloths moistened with clean, cool water instead of tea bags. Rest lightly over closed eyes without pressure. Avoid ice and frozen cloths. Stop if uncomfortable, and wash the cloths after each use.",
    "Evening Comfort Mask: rinse it off before bed. Don't sleep with the mixture on your face. Keep it away from your eyes, use a headband to keep hair clear, and discard leftovers.",
    "Neck & Chest Comfort Cream: make a fresh single-use portion and patch test any optional rosehip seed oil. Use light strokes without tugging or pressing on the front of your neck. Apply broad-spectrum SPF 30+ to exposed neck and chest every day.",
    "Soft-Lip Balm: use cosmetic-grade ingredients and a clean, dry spatula. Keep water out, let any melted mixture cool fully before application, and use a fresh single-use portion. The original 30-day storage claim isn't verified. Your usual fragrance-free lip balm is an alternative.",
    "Gentle Oat & Rice Polish: skip it with sensitive skin, inflamed acne, cuts, irritation, or products that increase skin sensitivity. Use only finely ground ingredients in a soft, well-moistened paste. Avoid scrubbing, pressure, and gritty mixtures.",
    "Creamy Rice Mask: avoid the eye area, and rinse immediately if skin feels tight, itchy, or uncomfortable. Make a fresh single-use portion and discard leftovers.",
    "5-Minute Gentle Face Massage: use a very light touch and enough oil for slip. Avoid tugging, irritated areas, and pressure on the front of your neck. Stop if uncomfortable. Massage doesn't provide a lasting lift or change skin structure.",
    "Fresh-Face Mist: this version replaces rose water with freshly poured distilled water to avoid added fragrance and botanical exposure. Make a single-use portion, avoid your eyes and inhalation, and follow with moisturizer. Discard leftovers and wash and dry the container.",
    "Creamy Yogurt Mask: skip it if you have a milk allergy. For oily skin, leave on for no more than 10 minutes, as tolerated. Rinse immediately if it stings. Use a fresh single-use portion and discard leftovers.",
    "Evening Comfort Oil: patch test rosehip seed oil before use and skip it if your skin reacts. Keep it away from eyes. Apply broad-spectrum SPF 30+ every morning, whether or not you use this oil.",
    "If skin feels tight, burns, itches, or looks red, rinse gently with cool to lukewarm water and stop using the mixture. Pause the Challenge and contact a dermatologist if symptoms persist or you're unsure. Get urgent medical help for trouble breathing or swelling of the lips or tongue.",
    "Introduce one new mixture at a time and allow adequate testing before adding another. Avoid combining homemade masks or the polish with retinoids, exfoliating acids, or other potentially irritating products. Ask your dermatologist about use alongside prescription skincare.",
    "Descriptions refer only to skin's appearance and feel, such as temporary softness, a fresh look, or comfort. Results vary, and some people may notice no benefit. No lasting wrinkle reduction, lifting, or age-related change is promised.",
    "This is informational cosmetic self-care, not medical advice or a substitute for a dermatologist's guidance. The recipes are loosely inspired by Korean-style skincare routines and don't represent specific authentic traditions. Quantities are estimates based on the ebook. The referenced recipes aren't reproduced here; apply this version's substitutions, testing precautions, and fresh-portion guidance wherever they differ from the ebook. Homemade mixtures haven't been tested for stability or microbial safety. Refrigeration, clean tools, and normal smell or appearance don't establish safety, and preservatives in packaged aloe don't reliably preserve a diluted mixture. Make fresh single-use portions and discard leftovers."
  ]
